    - target: {fileID: 122303113302768426, guid: e1f61480c48be6649b34ed19054e9e37, type: 3}
      propertyPath: 'lines.Array.data[0]'
      value: Just beyond is your prize. (press 'SPACE' to skip)
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 122303113302768426, guid: e1f61480c48be6649b34ed19054e9e37, type: 3}
      propertyPath: 'lines.Array.data[1]'
      value: Enter in your own peril!
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 122303113302768426, guid: e1f61480c48be6649b34ed19054e9e37, type: 3}
      propertyPath: 'lines.Array.data[2]'
      value: "Add a new built-in alias command that allows you to define a shortcut
        for\r\ncommands by essentially defining a new command that substitutes a
        given\r\nstring for some command, perhaps with various flags/options. The
        syntax is as\r\nfollows: alias alias_name='command'. For example, you can
        define an\r\nalias with alias 5='ls \u2013al', so that the user can then
        enter 5 at the prompt\r\nto execute the ls -al command. Although there is
        an alias command in\r\nbash, you must implement your own version and not
        make use of the built-in\r\nalias bash command. Typically, alias is a built-in
        command, but since this\r\naffects how your shell functions (i.e., you simply
        cannot just pass an aliased\r\ncommand to the exec family system call as
        you are managing the execution\r\nwhich would otherwise result in a not found
        message.\r\nSpecifying alias with no arguments should display a list of all
        existing aliases.\r\nYou may remove a single alias with the command alias
        -r alias_name or\r\nall defined aliases with alias -c. Be sure to handle
        the case if a user enters\r\nthe alias command incorrectly. You can perform
        a man alias for help in\r\nunderstanding how this built-in command is used,
        but only the functionality\r\nspecified here is required"
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 836845979256909009, guid: e1f61480c48be6649b34ed19054e9e37, type: 3}
      propertyPath: m_IsActive
      value: 1
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
